# Production build configuration, committed on purpose.
#
# VITE_API_URL is compiled INTO the client bundle at build time, so it is
# public by construction — anyone can read it in devtools on the live site.
# It is a public endpoint, not a credential, and belongs in version control
# next to the code that depends on it. Real secrets (JWT_SECRET,
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY) live in Railway and must never
# appear here.
#
# Why a file rather than a dashboard variable: Cloudflare Pages scopes
# variables per environment, and a value set on Production does not apply to
# Preview builds. That split silently broke two builds — the first Git-
# connected deploy and the first pull-request preview — each failing on the
# vite.config.ts guard with the value sitting right there in the other tab.
# A committed file applies to every build: Production, Preview, CI, and a
# fresh clone on a new machine.
#
# The guard in vite.config.ts stays and still earns its place. It now catches
# the real failure — no value anywhere — rather than an empty dashboard tab.
#
# Overriding is still possible and still wins: a Pages environment variable
# takes precedence over this file, and .env.production.local (gitignored via
# .env.*.local) overrides it locally. A future staging API therefore needs no
# change here.
VITE_API_URL=https://api.erestyu.com

# OAuth client ID for "Continue with Google" (google-signin.md). A client ID
# is PUBLIC by design (it ships in every browser bundle regardless), so
# committing it here is deliberate, not a leak. Absent -> the button simply
# does not render, mirroring the backend's 503 when its GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is
# unset.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=954819938801-j1v5ohi1c2fhael2rn164rafdp8r7cnc.apps.googleusercontent.com
